\begin{abstract}
We give a self-contained algebraic development of the Weil pairing on the $n$-torsion of an elliptic curve, and of the pairing-based BLS signature scheme built on it, in which every cryptographic property is derived from a minimal set of axioms: bi-additivity together with alternation, $e(P,P)=1$.

Our first group of results is structural. We show that on a group generated by two elements, alternation and bi-additivity \emph{force} the determinant form, $e(ae_1+be_2, ce_1+de_2) = \zeta^{ad-bc}$ with $\zeta = e(e_1,e_2)$. Over the model group $(\Z/n)^2$ with values in $\mu_n$ we obtain a complete classification: every alternating pairing is $\zeta^{u\det}$ for a unique scalar $u \in \Z/n$; it is nondegenerate if and only if $u \in (\Z/n)^\times$; and every nondegenerate alternating pairing is the pullback of the determinant pairing along a group automorphism (symplectic normal form). We prove a \emph{rank obstruction}: a nondegenerate alternating pairing on cyclic torsion forces the torsion to be trivial, so rank two is a theorem rather than a convention. Conversely, transporting the determinant form along any isomorphism $E[n]\cong(\Z/n)^2$ produces a genuine Weil pairing, establishing consistency and non-vacuity of the axioms.

Our second group of results is cryptanalytic: the degree law $e(mP,mQ)=e(P,Q)^{m^2}$ and, more generally, that a torsion endomorphism with matrix $\binom{a\ b}{c\ d}$ scales the pairing by $ad-bc$; the MOV embedding, that $a\mapsto e(aP,Q)$ is injective modulo $n$ when $\ord e(P,Q)=n$; and the fact that decisional Diffie--Hellman is \emph{decided} by a publicly computable pairing test, while that test is vacuous on a single cyclic subgroup.

Our third group of results concerns BLS signatures. We exhibit an obstruction: the natural symmetric model, positing one generator $G$ and injectivity of $P\mapsto e(P,G)$, is satisfiable only when the $n$-torsion is trivial, so every theorem proved in it is vacuous. We then develop the full theory over a corrected, inhabited asymmetric model with two generators $g_1,g_2$ and the requirement $\ord e(g_1,g_2)=n$: correctness; an exact characterisation of accepted signatures as the congruence class $s \equiv sk\cdot h \pmod n$, hence uniqueness; an unforgeability reduction in which a forgery on a programmed fresh message \emph{equals} the computational Diffie--Hellman target; aggregate correctness and uniqueness; aggregate-to-single forgery extraction with a tight reduction to computational Diffie--Hellman; soundness and completeness of weighted batch verification; an exact compression statement $N^m \twoheadrightarrow N$; and an explicit rogue-key attack showing the registered-key hypothesis cannot be dropped.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords.} Weil pairing; alternating bilinear form; symplectic normal form; elliptic curve torsion; BLS signatures; aggregate signatures; computational Diffie--Hellman; MOV reduction; rogue-key attack.

\section{Introduction}

Pairings occupy an unusual position in cryptography. They are simultaneously the enabling mechanism for a family of protocols with no known alternative --- identity-based encryption, short signatures, succinct arguments --- and the enabling mechanism for the most effective structural attack on the elliptic-curve discrete logarithm problem. Both roles derive from a single equation,
\[
e(aP, bQ) = e(P,Q)^{ab},
\]
which converts an unknown product of secret exponents into a publicly checkable equality.

The Weil pairing on the $n$-torsion $E[n]$ of an elliptic curve is classically constructed from divisors: one takes functions $f_P, f_Q$ with divisors $n(P)-n(O)$ and $n(Q)-n(O)$ and forms $e_n(P,Q) = f_P(D_Q)/f_Q(D_P)$. That construction is geometric, and its properties are proved by function-field arguments.

The present paper takes the opposite route. We work axiomatically, with the weakest possible interface, and ask how much of the theory is \emph{forced}. The answer is: essentially all of it. The determinant, which never appears in the axioms, is a theorem; and the rank-two structure of the torsion, usually imported from the classical structure theorem, is also a theorem. This is not merely aesthetic: because the derivations are short, they can be checked against the security definitions they support --- and doing so uncovers that the most natural way to state the BLS security model is inconsistent.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Forcing of the determinant form (Theorem~\ref{thm:det}) and the complete classification of alternating pairings on $(\Z/n)^2$ (Theorems~\ref{thm:class}, \ref{thm:nd}, \ref{thm:snf}).
\item A rank obstruction (Theorem~\ref{thm:rank}): nondegeneracy is incompatible with cyclic torsion.
\item Existence (Theorem~\ref{thm:exist}): the axioms are inhabited.
\item Cryptanalysis: degree law and endomorphism equivariance (Theorems~\ref{thm:degree}, \ref{thm:endo}), MOV embedding (Theorem~\ref{thm:mov}), and the collapse of decisional Diffie--Hellman (Theorem~\ref{thm:ddh}) with its cyclic blind spot (Proposition~\ref{prop:blindddh}).
\item An obstruction theorem for the symmetric BLS model (Theorem~\ref{thm:obstruction}).
\item The corrected asymmetric model and the full BLS theory over it (Sections~\ref{sec:bls}--\ref{sec:rogue}).
\item Exhaustive finite verification of the key identities (Section~\ref{sec:numeric}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Setting and notation}

Throughout, $A$ denotes an additively written abelian group and $\mu$ a multiplicatively written commutative group. For a Weierstrass curve $W$ over a field $F$ and $n\in\N$ we write $E[n]=\{P : nP=O\}$ for the $n$-torsion subgroup. We write $\Z/n$ for the integers modulo $n$ and $\mu_n$ for a cyclic group of order dividing $n$; concretely $\mu_n$ may be realised as $\Z/n$ written multiplicatively. $\ord(x)$ denotes the order of a group element.

\section{Alternating pairings and the forcing of the determinant}

\begin{definition}[Alternating pairing]
An \emph{alternating pairing} on $A$ with values in $\mu$ is a map $e:A\times A\to\mu$, additive in each argument,
\[
e(x+y,z)=e(x,z)e(y,z),\qquad e(x,y+z)=e(x,y)e(x,z),
\]
satisfying the \emph{alternation} axiom $e(x,x)=1$ for all $x$.
\end{definition}

Bi-additivity alone yields $e(0,y)=e(x,0)=1$ and, by induction and passage to inverses, full $\Z$-bilinearity:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:bilin}
e(kx,y)=e(x,y)^k=e(x,ky)\qquad(k\in\Z).
\end{equation}

\begin{proposition}[Skew-symmetry]\label{prop:skew}
For all $x,y$ we have $e(y,x)=e(x,y)^{-1}$ and $e(x-y,z)=e(x,z)e(y,z)^{-1}$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Expand $1=e(x+y,x+y)$ by bi-additivity into $e(x,x)e(x,y)e(y,x)e(y,y)$. The outer factors are $1$ by alternation, so $e(x,y)e(y,x)=1$. The second identity follows from \eqref{eq:bilin} with $k=-1$ and additivity.
\end{proof}

Antisymmetry is derived, not assumed; over groups of even order the two conditions genuinely differ, alternation being the stronger.

\begin{proposition}[Blindness on cyclic subgroups]\label{prop:blind}
For all $a,b\in\Z$ and $x\in A$, $e(ax,bx)=1$. Consequently an alternating pairing on a cyclic group is identically $1$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
By \eqref{eq:bilin} twice, $e(ax,bx)=e(x,x)^{ab}=1$. If $A=\langle g\rangle$ write $x=ag$, $y=bg$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The determinant is forced]\label{thm:det}
Let $e_1,e_2\in A$ and put $\zeta=e(e_1,e_2)$. Then for all integers $a,b,c,d$,
\[
e\big(ae_1+be_2,\; ce_1+de_2\big)=\zeta^{\,ad-bc}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Expand both arguments by bi-additivity into
$e(ae_1,ce_1)\,e(ae_1,de_2)\,e(be_2,ce_1)\,e(be_2,de_2)$.
The first and fourth factors are $1$ by Proposition~\ref{prop:blind}. By \eqref{eq:bilin} the second is $\zeta^{ad}$, and the third is $e(e_2,e_1)^{bc}=\zeta^{-bc}$ by Proposition~\ref{prop:skew}. Multiplying gives $\zeta^{ad-bc}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Rigidity]
Two alternating pairings agreeing on a pair $(e_1,e_2)$ agree on all of $\langle e_1,e_2\rangle$.
\end{corollary}

Theorem~\ref{thm:det} is the technical heart of the paper: all that follows is an application, a specialisation, or a consequence of the fact that its exponent is a determinant.

\section{Complete classification over \texorpdfstring{$(\Z/n)^2$}{(Z/n)^2}}

Fix $n\ge 1$ and work on $A=(\Z/n)^2$ with values in $\mu_n\cong\Z/n$, with standard basis $\varepsilon_1=(1,0)$, $\varepsilon_2=(0,1)$.

\begin{definition}[Determinant pairing]
The \emph{determinant form} is $\det(v,w)=v_1w_2-v_2w_1\in\Z/n$, and the \emph{determinant pairing} is $e_{\det}(v,w)=\zeta_0^{\det(v,w)}$ with $\zeta_0$ the canonical generator of $\mu_n$. It is bi-additive, alternating and $n$-torsion valued.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Classification]\label{thm:class}
Let $e$ be any alternating pairing on $(\Z/n)^2$ valued in $\mu_n$, and let $u\in\Z/n$ satisfy $e(\varepsilon_1,\varepsilon_2)=\zeta_0^{u}$. Then $e(v,w)=\zeta_0^{\,u\det(v,w)}$ for all $v,w$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Write $v=v_1\varepsilon_1+v_2\varepsilon_2$, $w=w_1\varepsilon_1+w_2\varepsilon_2$ with integer representatives. Theorem~\ref{thm:det} gives $e(v,w)=e(\varepsilon_1,\varepsilon_2)^{v_1w_2-v_2w_1}$; substitute and reduce the exponent modulo $n$, legitimate since $\ord\zeta_0 \mid n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Nondegeneracy]
$e$ is \emph{nondegenerate on the left} if $e(v,w)=1$ for all $w$ implies $v=0$; nondegeneracy on the right is equivalent by skew-symmetry.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Nondegeneracy criterion]\label{thm:nd}
With $u$ as above, $e$ is nondegenerate if and only if $u\in(\Z/n)^\times$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) If $u$ is invertible, $e(v,w)=1$ for all $w$ gives $\det(v,w)=0$ for all $w$; taking $w=\varepsilon_2$ gives $v_1=0$ and $w=\varepsilon_1$ gives $-v_2=0$. (This also proves $e_{\det}$ nondegenerate, the case $u=1$.)

($\Rightarrow$) If $u$ is not a unit, let $g=\gcd(\tilde u,n)>1$ for an integer representative $\tilde u$ and put $k=n/g$, so $0<k<n$ and $ku=0$ in $\Z/n$. Then $v=(k,0)\ne 0$ but $u\det(v,w)=u\,k\,w_2=0$ for all $w$, so $e(v,\cdot)\equiv 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Counting]
The nondegenerate alternating pairings on $(\Z/n)^2$ valued in $\mu_n$ are in bijection with $(\Z/n)^\times$: there are $\varphi(n)$ of them, exactly $n-1$ when $n$ is prime, and they are the primitive powers of the determinant pairing.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Symplectic normal form]\label{thm:snf}
Let $e$ be nondegenerate alternating on $(\Z/n)^2$. Then there is a group automorphism $\psi$ with $e(v,w)=e_{\det}(\psi v, w)$ for all $v,w$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:nd} the scalar $u$ is a unit; let $\psi(v)=(uv_1,uv_2)$, an automorphism with inverse given by $u^{-1}$. Then $\det(\psi v,w)=u\det(v,w)$ and Theorem~\ref{thm:class} concludes.
\end{proof}

Thus the Weil pairing is unique up to symplectic isomorphism. Methodologically: to identify any concrete construction with the determinant pairing, one need only compute a \emph{single} value on a basis pair.

\begin{theorem}[Nondegeneracy pins the root of unity]\label{thm:root}
If $e$ is alternating on $(\Z/n)^2$, $n$-torsion valued and nondegenerate, then $\zeta:=e(\varepsilon_1,\varepsilon_2)$ has exact order $n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $k=\ord\zeta$, so $k\mid n$. The element $v=(k,0)$ satisfies $e(v,w)=\zeta^{kw_2}=1$ for all $w$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:det}. Nondegeneracy forces $v=0$, i.e.\ $n\mid k$, so $k=n$.
\end{proof}

\section{Rank obstruction and existence}

\begin{definition}[Weil pairing]
A \emph{Weil pairing} on $E[n]$ with values in $\mu$ is an alternating pairing that is $n$-torsion valued, $e(P,Q)^n=1$, and nondegenerate on both sides.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Rank obstruction]\label{thm:rank}
If $E[n]$ is cyclic, generated by $g$, and carries a Weil pairing, then $E[n]=0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:blind} the pairing is identically $1$; in particular $e(g,Q)=1$ for all $Q$, so left nondegeneracy gives $g=0$, and every element is a multiple of $g$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
If some $n$-torsion point is nonzero and $E[n]$ carries a Weil pairing, then $E[n]$ is not cyclic.
\end{corollary}

This is the intrinsic reason pairing-based cryptography is two-dimensional: nontrivial nondegenerate alternation cannot exist in rank one. Symmetric (type-1) pairings require a distortion map precisely because $e$ restricted to $\langle P\rangle\times\langle P\rangle$ is trivial.

\begin{lemma}[Transport]\label{lem:transport}
If $\varphi:A\to B$ is a group homomorphism and $e$ an alternating pairing on $B$, then $(x,y)\mapsto e(\varphi x,\varphi y)$ is alternating on $A$; if $\varphi$ is an isomorphism and $e$ nondegenerate, so is the transported pairing.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Bi-additivity and alternation are immediate. If $e(\varphi x,\varphi y)=1$ for all $y$ then, by surjectivity, $e(\varphi x,w)=1$ for all $w\in B$, so $\varphi x=0$ and $x=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Existence / non-vacuity]\label{thm:exist}
If $E[n]\cong(\Z/n)^2$ as abelian groups, then $E[n]$ carries a Weil pairing valued in $\mu_n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Transport the determinant pairing along the isomorphism (Lemma~\ref{lem:transport}); alternation and $n$-torsion values are inherited and nondegeneracy follows from Theorem~\ref{thm:nd} with $u=1$.
\end{proof}

The classical structure theorem supplies the hypothesis whenever $\operatorname{char} F \nmid n$. Theorem~\ref{thm:exist} certifies that the axiom system is consistent, so no subsequent theorem is vacuous.

\section{Cryptanalytic consequences}

\begin{theorem}[Degree law]\label{thm:degree}
For all $m\in\Z$ and $x,y\in A$, $e(mx,my)=e(x,y)^{m^2}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply \eqref{eq:bilin} in each slot.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Endomorphism equivariance]\label{thm:endo}
On $(\Z/n)^2$ let $\varphi$ have matrix $M=\binom{a\ \ b}{c\ \ d}$. Then $\det(\varphi v,\varphi w)=(ad-bc)\det(v,w)$ and $e_{\det}(\varphi v,\varphi w)=e_{\det}(v,w)^{\det M}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Expand $(av_1+bv_2)(cw_1+dw_2)-(cv_1+dv_2)(aw_1+bw_2)=(ad-bc)(v_1w_2-v_2w_1)$.
\end{proof}

Taking $M=mI$ recovers Theorem~\ref{thm:degree} with $\det M=m^2$. Theorem~\ref{thm:endo} is the algebraic shadow of $e(\varphi P,\varphi Q)=e(P,Q)^{\deg\varphi}$ for an isogeny $\varphi$.

\begin{theorem}[MOV embedding]\label{thm:mov}
Suppose $\zeta=e(P,Q)$ has exact order $n$. Then $a\mapsto e(aP,Q)=\zeta^a$ is injective on $\{0,\dots,n-1\}$; consequently if $b<n$ satisfies $\zeta^{b}=e(aP,Q)$ with $a<n$, then $bP=aP$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$e(aP,Q)=\zeta^a$ by \eqref{eq:bilin}, and $x\mapsto\zeta^x$ is injective on a set of representatives of size $\ord\zeta=n$.
\end{proof}

The security significance: the existence of a nondegenerate pairing valued in $\mu_n \subseteq \mathbb{F}_{q^k}^\times$ transports the elliptic-curve discrete logarithm into a finite field where index-calculus attacks apply, so the embedding degree $k$ must be chosen large enough for security and small enough for efficiency.

\begin{theorem}[Decisional Diffie--Hellman is easy]\label{thm:ddh}
Suppose $\zeta=e(P,Q)$ has exact order $n$. Then for all $a,b,c\in\N$,
\[
e(aP,bQ)=e(P,cQ)\iff ab\equiv c \pmod n,
\]
and both sides are computable from the group elements $aP$, $bQ$, $cQ$ alone.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The two sides are $\zeta^{ab}$ and $\zeta^{c}$; equality holds iff the exponents agree modulo $\ord\zeta=n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
No security proof in a pairing group may rest on the decisional Diffie--Hellman assumption. This is why the scheme below is reduced to the \emph{computational} problem: given $a\,g_2$ and $h\,g_1$, produce $ah\,g_1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[The test is blind on a cyclic subgroup]\label{prop:blindddh}
For any $P$ and $a,b,c$, $e(aP,bP)=1=e(P,cP)$, so the test of Theorem~\ref{thm:ddh} is uninformative when all inputs lie in $\langle P\rangle$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Proposition~\ref{prop:blind}.
\end{proof}

\section{An obstruction in the naive signature model}

\begin{definition}[Symmetric model with injective verification]
A \emph{symmetric BLS setup} consists of an alternating pairing $e$ on $E[n]$, a distinguished generator $G$, and the requirement that $P\mapsto e(P,G)$ be injective.
\end{definition}

The requirement is attractive: it makes signature uniqueness immediate. It is also self-contradictory.

\begin{theorem}[Obstruction]\label{thm:obstruction}
In any symmetric BLS setup, $G=0$ and every $Q\in E[n]$ is $0$. Hence such a setup exists only over curves with trivial $n$-torsion, and every statement proved from these axioms is vacuous.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By alternation $e(G,G)=1$ and by bi-additivity $e(0,G)=1$; injectivity gives $G=0$. Then for any $Q$, $e(Q,G)=e(Q,0)=1=e(0,G)$, so $Q=0$.
\end{proof}

The axiom is a strengthened nondegeneracy: nondegeneracy asserts only that $P\mapsto e(P,\cdot)$ is injective into the dual, whereas here the second argument is fixed at $G$ --- an element that pairs trivially with itself. The general lesson: a model bundling an injectivity assumption with an alternating form must be checked for inhabitedness before anything is proved in it.

\section{The corrected asymmetric setting and single-signer signatures}\label{sec:bls}

\begin{definition}[Signature setting]
A \emph{signature setting} consists of an abelian group $A$, a commutative group $\mu$, an alternating pairing $e$ on $A$ valued in $\mu$, elements $g_1$ (signature generator) and $g_2$ (key generator), and the requirements
\[
n\,g_1=0,\qquad \ord\big(e(g_1,g_2)\big)=n .
\]
We write $\zeta=e(g_1,g_2)$ for the \emph{root of unity} of the setting.
\end{definition}

This is the asymmetric (type-3) configuration used in practice. By Theorem~\ref{thm:root} the order condition is exactly what nondegeneracy delivers; by Theorem~\ref{thm:obstruction} everything has been gained, since --- unlike the symmetric model --- this setting is inhabited (Theorem~\ref{thm:inhab}).

\begin{definition}[Scheme]
For $sk,h\in\N$: the public key is $pk(sk)=sk\cdot g_2$; the hash point is $H(h)=h\cdot g_1$; the signature is $\sigma(sk,h)=(sk\,h)\cdot g_1$; and verification is
\[
\Ver(pk,H,\sigma)\iff e(\sigma,g_2)=e(H,pk).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Pairing values]\label{lem:vals}
$e(a g_1,b g_2)=\zeta^{ab}$, $e(a g_1,g_2)=\zeta^{a}$, and $e(H(h),pk(sk))=\zeta^{sk\,h}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
\eqref{eq:bilin} in each slot.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Correctness and exact verification criterion]\label{thm:correct}
For all $sk,h,s\in\N$,
\[
\Ver\big(pk(sk),H(h),s\cdot g_1\big)\iff s\equiv sk\,h \pmod n .
\]
In particular the honest signature verifies.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:vals} the equation reads $\zeta^{s}=\zeta^{sk\,h}$, equivalent to $s\equiv sk\,h \bmod \ord\zeta = n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness of signatures]\label{thm:unique}
If $\Ver(pk(sk),H(h),s\cdot g_1)$ then $s\cdot g_1=\sigma(sk,h)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:correct}, $s\equiv sk\,h \bmod n$. Since $n g_1=0$, writing $c=qn+r$ gives $c\,g_1=r\,g_1$, so congruent exponents act identically.
\end{proof}

Signatures are therefore unique, hence deterministic: there is no nonce to randomise and none to leak.

\begin{definition}[Fresh-message game]
A \emph{fresh game} consists of a secret exponent $a$ (the signer's key and the CDH secret), a hash-exponent programming function $h(\cdot)$, a target message $m^*$, a finite set $Q$ of already-signed messages, and the freshness condition $m^*\notin Q$. Its \emph{CDH target} is
\[
T=a\cdot H\big(h(m^*)\big)=\big(a\,h(m^*)\big)g_1 .
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[A forgery \emph{is} the CDH solution]\label{thm:forge}
In a fresh game with secret $a$, if $s\cdot g_1$ is accepted for $pk(a)$ and $H(h(m^*))$, then $s\cdot g_1=T$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:unique}, $s\,g_1=\sigma(a,h(m^*))=(a\,h(m^*))g_1=T$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Existential unforgeability under CDH]\label{thm:euf}
Let $\mathcal R\subseteq A$ be the adversary's attainable set. If $T\notin\mathcal R$, then no $s$ satisfies both $s\,g_1\in\mathcal R$ and $\Ver(pk(a),H(h(m^*)),s\,g_1)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Such an $s$ would give $s\,g_1=T\in\mathcal R$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:forge}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The reduction is tight in the strongest possible sense: the forgery is literally the challenge answer --- no factor is stripped and no rewinding is used.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Inhabitedness]\label{thm:inhab}
(i) For every $n\ge 1$, $(\Z/n)^2$ with the determinant pairing, $g_1=\varepsilon_1$, $g_2=\varepsilon_2$, is a signature setting. (ii) Every Weierstrass curve with $E[n]\cong(\Z/n)^2$ carries a signature setting, with the pairing of Theorem~\ref{thm:exist} and the images of the standard basis as generators.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
(i) $n\varepsilon_1=0$, and $\ord e_{\det}(\varepsilon_1,\varepsilon_2)=n$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:root}. (ii) Transport along the isomorphism; the pairing value on the transported basis equals the value on the standard basis.
\end{proof}

\section{Aggregation}

\begin{definition}[Aggregate signature and verification]
For a finite signer set $S$, keys $pk_i$, hash points $H_i$ and signatures $\sigma_i$, the \emph{aggregate} is $\sigma=\sum_{i\in S}\sigma_i$, and
\[
\AggVer(S,pk,H,\sigma)\iff e(\sigma,g_2)=\prod_{i\in S}e(H_i,pk_i).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Aggregate correctness]\label{thm:aggcorrect}
With $pk_i=pk(sk_i)$, $H_i=H(h_i)$ and honest $\sigma_i$, the aggregate $\sum_{i\in S}\sigma_i$ verifies.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By induction on $S$ and additivity in the first slot, $e(\sum_i c_i g_1,g_2)=\zeta^{\sum_i c_i}$; with $c_i=sk_i h_i$ this is $\zeta^{\sum_i sk_i h_i}$, which equals $\prod_i \zeta^{sk_ih_i}$, the right-hand side by Lemma~\ref{lem:vals}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Aggregate uniqueness]
In the registered-key setting, any aggregate candidate in the signature group satisfying the verification equation equals $\sum_{i\in S}\sigma(sk_i,h_i)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Both satisfy the same equation, so their pairings against $g_2$ agree, so their exponents agree modulo $n=\ord\zeta$, so they are equal in $\langle g_1\rangle$ as in Theorem~\ref{thm:unique}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Aggregate-to-single forgery extraction]\label{thm:extract}
Let $i_0\in S$, let $\sigma=t\,g_1$ satisfy the aggregate verification equation for the
data $(S,pk,H)$, and suppose the co-signer keys $sk_i$, $i\ne i_0$, are known. Then
\[
\sigma'=\sigma-\sum_{i\in S\setminus\{i_0\}}\sigma(sk_i,h_i)
\]
satisfies $\Ver\big(pk(sk_{i_0}),H(h_{i_0}),\sigma'\big)$. No assumption is made about how $\sigma$ was produced.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Aggregate verification gives $\zeta^{t}=\zeta^{\sum_{i\in S}sk_ih_i}$. Split $\sum_{i\in S}sk_ih_i=sk_{i_0}h_{i_0}+\Sigma$ with $\Sigma=\sum_{i\ne i_0}sk_ih_i$; the co-signer sum pairs to $\zeta^{\Sigma}$. By Proposition~\ref{prop:skew}, subtraction in the first slot divides:
\[
e(\sigma',g_2)=e(\sigma,g_2)\zeta^{-\Sigma}=\zeta^{t-\Sigma}=\zeta^{sk_{i_0}h_{i_0}}=e\big(H(h_{i_0}),pk(sk_{i_0})\big).\qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Aggregate unforgeability reduces to CDH]\label{thm:aggcdh}
In a fresh game with secret $a$ and target message $m^*$, suppose $sk_{i_0}=a$ and $H_{i_0}=H(h(m^*))$. If $\sigma$ satisfies $\AggVer(S,pk,H,\sigma)$ then
\[
\sigma-\sum_{i\in S\setminus\{i_0\}}\sigma(sk_i,h_i)=T .
\]
Consequently, if $T$ is unattainable and the attainable set is closed under subtracting the known co-signer contributions, no attainable $\sigma$ satisfies the aggregate equation.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:extract} produces a valid single-signer signature on the programmed fresh message; Theorem~\ref{thm:forge} identifies it with $T$. The second claim is the contrapositive.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The reduction consumes exactly two hypotheses: freshness of the target message, so that the signing oracle can be simulated, and knowledge of the co-signers' keys, so that the extraction is computable. The latter is the knowledge-of-secret-key (proof-of-possession) assumption, shown indispensable in Section~\ref{sec:rogue}.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Batch verification: soundness and completeness]\label{thm:batch}
For weights $r$ define
\[
\Batch(S,pk,H,\sigma,r)\iff e\Big(\sum_{i\in S}r_i\sigma_i,\;g_2\Big)=\prod_{i\in S}e(H_i,pk_i)^{r_i}.
\]
Then $\Batch$ holds for \emph{all} weight vectors $r$ if and only if $\Ver(pk_i,H_i,\sigma_i)$ holds for every $i\in S$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) By additivity and \eqref{eq:bilin}, $e(\sum_i r_i\sigma_i,g_2)=\prod_i e(\sigma_i,g_2)^{r_i}$; substitute each individual equation. ($\Rightarrow$) Take indicator weights $r_j=[j=i_0]$: the sum collapses to $\sigma_{i_0}$ and the product to $e(H_{i_0},pk_{i_0})$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact compression]\label{thm:compress}
Let $A$ be finite with $|A|=N$ and let $m\ge 1$. Then the space of $m$-tuples of signatures has $N^m$ elements, while the set of values of the aggregation map $(\sigma_1,\dots,\sigma_m)\mapsto\sum_i\sigma_i$ has exactly $N$ elements. Hence aggregation is precisely an $N^m\twoheadrightarrow N$ compression.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The first claim counts functions. For the second, the image lies in $A$ so has at most $N$ elements; conversely $(g,0,\dots,0)$ aggregates to $g$, so the image is all of $A$.
\end{proof}

The statement matters because "the aggregate is one group element" does not by itself exclude the possibility that only a thin subset of the group is reachable, which would signal exploitable structure. The image is the whole group.

\section{The rogue-key attack}\label{sec:rogue}

\begin{definition}[Rogue key]
Given a victim public key $pk_1$ and any adversarial scalar $y$, the \emph{rogue public key} is $pk_2=y\,g_2-pk_1$, computable from $pk_1$ alone.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Plain aggregation is universally forgeable]\label{thm:rogue}
For every $y$, every $pk_1$ and every hash point $H$, the element $\sigma=y\cdot H$ satisfies the two-signer aggregate equation for $(pk_1,pk_2)$ on the common message $H$:
\[
e(yH,g_2)=e(H,pk_1)\,e(H,pk_2).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By additivity in the second slot,
\[
e(H,pk_1)e(H,pk_2)=e\big(H,\;pk_1+(y g_2-pk_1)\big)=e(H,y g_2)=e(H,g_2)^y=e(yH,g_2).\qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

The forger uses no secret key and no interaction with the victim, and the identity is unconditional --- there is no failure case.

\begin{corollary}
The knowledge-of-secret-key hypothesis in Theorems~\ref{thm:extract} and \ref{thm:aggcdh} cannot be removed. Deployments must require proofs of possession at registration, or use a defended aggregation rule (for instance exponentiating each key by a hash of the key set), which blocks the algebraic cancellation above.
\end{corollary}

\section{Computational verification}\label{sec:numeric}

All identities were checked exhaustively on small models, by enumeration over the entire parameter space rather than by sampling.

\begin{center}
\small
\begin{tabular}{llll}
\toprule
Check & Model & Cases & Result\\
\midrule
Nondegeneracy of $\det$ & $(\Z/5)^2$ & $625$ pairs & holds\\
Skew-symmetry, composite modulus & $(\Z/6)^2$ & $1296$ pairs & holds\\
Cyclic blindness $\det(av,bv)=0$ & $(\Z/5)^2$ & $625$ & holds\\
Endomorphism law $\det(Mv,Mw)=\det(M)\det(v,w)$ & $(\Z/3)^2$ & $81\times 81$ & holds\\
Rogue-key identity & $(\Z/5)^2$ & all $y,H,pk$ & holds\\
Signature correctness & $(\Z/7)^2$ & $49$ & holds\\
Exact criterion $s\equiv sk\,h \ (7)$ & $(\Z/7)^2$ & $343$ & holds, both directions\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The last row is the numerical face of Theorem~\ref{thm:correct}: among the seven candidate signatures $s\,g_1$, exactly one is accepted, and it is the honest one.

\section{Discussion}

\textbf{What is assumed and what is derived.} The development assumes only bi-additivity and $e(P,P)=1$. From these, skew-symmetry, cyclic blindness, the determinant form, the classification, the rank obstruction, the degree law, the MOV embedding and the DDH collapse are all theorems. The cryptographic layer adds one structural hypothesis --- that $\zeta=e(g_1,g_2)$ has exact order $n$ --- and one computational assumption, hardness of computational Diffie--Hellman.

\textbf{On adversarial self-review.} Theorem~\ref{thm:obstruction} is the methodological centrepiece. A security model can be internally consistent as prose and uninhabited as mathematics; when uninhabited, every theorem proved in it is true and worthless. The failure survives informal review precisely because each ingredient is individually standard. The remedy is cheap: exhibit a model before proving anything.

\textbf{On tightness.} The forgery \emph{equals} the CDH target, a consequence of signature uniqueness, which is in turn a consequence of the exact-order hypothesis. Randomised schemes do not enjoy this and pay with looser reductions.

\textbf{Limitations.} We treat the hash-to-curve map as programmable with known exponents, i.e.\ we work in the random-oracle idealisation. The adversary is modelled abstractly by an attainable-set predicate, adequate for the algebraic content but not a concrete-security analysis. The classification is stated over $(\Z/n)^2$. Finally, the divisor-theoretic pairing is not constructed here: Theorem~\ref{thm:snf} shows only that any nondegenerate alternating construction must coincide with the determinant model up to a unit.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Symplectic structure implies square order]
Every finite abelian group carrying a nondegenerate alternating pairing into a commutative group has square cardinality, and admits a symplectic basis $(x_1,y_1,\dots,x_g,y_g)$ with $e(x_i,y_j)=\zeta_i^{\delta_{ij}}$ and $e(x_i,x_j)=e(y_i,y_j)=1$.
\end{conjecture}

The rank obstruction (Theorem~\ref{thm:rank}) is exactly the rank-one base case of the induction that would prove this: choose $x$ of maximal order, use nondegeneracy to find $y$ with $e(x,y)$ of the same order, and split off the orthogonal complement of $\langle x,y\rangle$. The complement step is the only missing ingredient, and the structure theory of finite abelian groups supplies the decomposition against which to run the induction. It is falsifiable: exhibit a finite abelian group of non-square order with a nondegenerate alternating pairing, or show the induction step fails for $\Z/p\times\Z/p^2$.

\begin{conjecture}[Divisor-theoretic pairing matches the determinant model]
The classical Weil pairing $e_n(P,Q)=f_P(D_Q)/f_Q(D_P)$ is, after any isomorphism $E[n]\cong(\Z/n)^2$, a unit multiple of the determinant pairing --- so the transported pairing of Theorem~\ref{thm:exist} is not merely \emph{a} pairing but \emph{the} Weil pairing, up to a choice of primitive root of unity.
\end{conjecture}

The symplectic normal form reduces this identification to a single computation: any bi-additive, alternating, nondegenerate construction is forced into determinant normal form, so only the value on \emph{one} basis pair need be computed from divisors.

Further directions: quantify the embedding-degree trade-off implied by Theorem~\ref{thm:mov}, relating $\ord\zeta$ to the field of definition of $\mu_n$; extend the aggregation theory to defended aggregation rules and prove the analogue of Theorem~\ref{thm:extract} without the knowledge-of-secret-key hypothesis; develop threshold and multi-signature variants in the same axiomatic frame, where uniqueness of signatures should yield unusually simple robustness arguments; and refine the compression statement to a statement about the \emph{distribution} of aggregates under honest generation, the quantity relevant to privacy rather than bandwidth.

\section{Conclusion}

A single axiom, $e(P,P)=1$, imposed on a bi-additive map determines the entire theory. It forces the determinant form and with it the classification of pairings up to a unit; it forces rank-two torsion if the pairing is to be nontrivial; it makes decisional Diffie--Hellman easy and thereby dictates reduction to the computational variant; it makes signatures unique and reductions tight; and it quietly destroys the most natural symmetric formulation of the signature model, whose injectivity axiom it contradicts outright. The corrected asymmetric setting is inhabited, supports the full theory including aggregation, and shows exactly where the registered-key hypothesis is load-bearing: remove it, and a rogue key breaks the scheme in one line.

